04:57.557 --> 05:00.768
these things, so it's important to be trained and understand kind of what our

05:00.849 --> 05:05.280
obligations are as members of the Planning Commission. So let's—

05:05.601 --> 05:08.877
all right. Okay. Open and

05:09.377 --> 05:12.586
Public Meetings Act. We're going to start there. So it is the intent of the

05:13.086 --> 05:16.646
legislature that the State, its agencies, and its political subdivisions

05:16.662 --> 05:20.304
take their actions openly and conduct their deliberations openly.

05:20.512 --> 05:23.817
So that is the public policy surrounding the Open and Public Meetings

05:23.881 --> 05:27.507
Act. The Planning Commission is subject to the act, and so

05:28.053 --> 05:31.422
you have to abide by it, and thus have to understand what it

05:31.454 --> 05:34.630
is that your obligations are. Okay,

05:34.646 --> 05:37.742
so the default rule is that a meeting is open

05:37.774 --> 05:41.561
to the public. As a planning commission, I think I'm aware of one time

05:42.061 --> 05:44.705
in our city's history where a planning commission has ever gone into closed. Session,

05:45.074 --> 05:48.459
or one incident that led to— may have been multiple meetings where they went into

05:48.507 --> 05:53.642
closed session, but it was regarding the same situation. So it's

05:54.142 --> 05:57.075
not something that's likely that's going to come up during your tenure, but it is

05:57.252 --> 06:00.412
possible, so it's good to understand how the procedure works.

06:01.022 --> 06:03.557
So again, the default rule is it's open to the public, and it can only

06:03.605 --> 06:06.830
be closed for very specific reasons under

06:07.330 --> 06:11.178
the statute, none of which are likely to apply. The one prior situation had

06:11.678 --> 06:15.478
to do with reasonably imminent litigation, which is one of the reasons, or pending litigation,

06:15.558 --> 06:18.702
which is one of the reasons that you may close a meeting.

06:18.863 --> 06:22.360
Requires a 2/3 supermajority vote, which in the case of

06:22.376 --> 06:24.461
5 voting members is 4 votes.

06:24.798 --> 06:27.991
Extremely unlikely you'd ever close a meeting, but could

06:28.491 --> 06:32.066
happen. Okay. So in order to understand this, you need to understand,

06:32.146 --> 06:35.852
one, the term meeting is actually a defined term in the statute. Here's the

06:36.352 --> 06:40.184
definition. I think I will skip reading it. You can

06:40.684 --> 06:44.003
read that first section yourself, but that is the basic definition.

06:44.517 --> 06:48.770
But the gist of it is a quorum, plus you're discussing public

06:49.270 --> 06:52.558
business that's within your jurisdiction, that is a meeting.

06:52.638 --> 06:56.153
It's a little bit more complicated than that, but that's the general gist

06:56.653 --> 06:59.571
of it. It does not have to be in person. It could be phone calls,

06:59.604 --> 07:02.252
video conferencing, emails, text messages, et cetera.

07:02.428 --> 07:05.590
It doesn't have to be any formal setting. A quorum is

07:05.703 --> 07:09.045
3 members of the body in your situation.

07:09.736 --> 07:13.015
So, because we have 5 voting members, it's a majority.

07:14.510 --> 07:18.739
OK. So here is an example of what not

07:22.303 --> 07:25.702
Members of the Commission, Mr. Vorstein is pleased to be here

07:26.202 --> 07:29.424
today. And when the day finally came, I was ready. I felt so

07:29.924 --> 07:33.625
confident that all I had to do was present my case. And we have documents,

07:33.641 --> 07:37.382
one of which is a report by retired FBI agents which

07:37.478 --> 07:40.947
completely absolves Mr. Rothstein from any wrongdoing. I'd like this

07:41.447 --> 07:43.965
marked. Pardon me, Counselor, before you continue. No, I want to have this marked.

07:44.062 --> 07:47.915
This, uh, this commission is prepared to act on a motion denying the Rothstein

07:48.415 --> 07:51.608
application. Denied! Do I hear a motion seconded? Mr. Chairman.

07:51.689 --> 07:54.595
Mr. Chairman, I second the motion. Do I have a vote on the motion?

07:54.675 --> 07:57.333
Mr. Chairman. Aye. Aye. Aye.

07:57.817 --> 07:59.603
The ayes have it. This hearing is adjourned.

08:01.470 --> 08:02.991
Okay. So here,

08:04.076 --> 08:07.378
obviously, you wouldn't think that the movie Casino is about

08:07.491 --> 08:09.806
Open Meetings Act, but here you'd be proven wrong.

08:11.150 --> 08:14.717
So the gist of it is that they

08:14.781 --> 08:17.722
obviously had already decided what they were going to do in advance,

08:17.850 --> 08:20.911
and this would be illegal under Utah law.

08:21.238 --> 08:24.824
So individuals constituting a quorum of a public body, So again,

08:24.921 --> 08:28.721
3 individuals in the case of the Planning Commission, may not act together outside

08:29.221 --> 08:32.311
a meeting in a concerted and deliberate way to predetermine an action to be taken

08:32.811 --> 08:36.340
by the public body at a meeting on a relevant matter. So relevant matter

08:36.421 --> 08:40.062
is a defined term in the statute. It means something that's within

08:40.562 --> 08:41.325
your jurisdiction to act on.

08:45.173 --> 08:48.717
So, okay. So again, here's some miscellaneous rules.

08:48.847 --> 08:51.887
Again, quorum is 3 members. Chance for social meetings.

08:53.302 --> 08:56.716
If you go bowling together and you don't talk about city business,

08:56.972 --> 09:00.049
that's, you know, Planning Commission business, that's fine.

09:00.257 --> 09:04.103
You can have all of you together going to social meetings. I wouldn't necessarily recommend

09:04.603 --> 09:08.094
it based on my own history and experience that those things sometimes—

09:08.606 --> 09:12.100
what do you have that's in common among you all? You're on the Planning Commission,

09:12.148 --> 09:14.984
so those types of subjects tend to come up. So I wouldn't necessarily recommend it,

09:15.016 --> 09:17.869
but in and of itself is not a violation of the Open and Public Meetings

09:17.965 --> 09:21.788
Act. But again, it states in the statute it cannot

09:22.288 --> 09:25.746
be used to circumvent the requirements of the statute. So you

09:26.246 --> 09:28.617
can't say, hey, let's go bowling and discuss this rezone.

09:30.630 --> 09:34.046
So, and the last thing that's kind of a miscellaneous

09:34.143 --> 09:38.359
rule is you can only take action on items that are properly noticed. So sometimes

09:38.715 --> 09:42.297
people come and they wanna, it's more common in city council

09:42.442 --> 09:45.929
meetings, but it could happen here. Where you wanna talk about, hey,

09:46.138 --> 09:49.605
this is something that we should put on the agenda, or here's something I prepared.

09:50.070 --> 09:54.371
I know in past Planning Commissions, there were some very proactive Planning Commissioners

09:54.420 --> 09:57.453
that would draft code that they wanted, you know,

09:57.517 --> 10:01.032
without reference to any staff members or their expertise,

10:01.129 --> 10:04.563
and they would want to run it through the Planning Commission and send

10:04.595 --> 10:07.805
it to City Council. So, I mean, that's,

10:08.030 --> 10:11.531
you can do that. But you can't just

10:12.031 --> 10:15.207
bring it up in a meeting. It would have to be noticed properly.

10:15.319 --> 10:19.044
So in the case of anything pretty much in your jurisdiction, it would

10:19.544 --> 10:22.515
be public hearing and would require a 10-day notice.

10:24.220 --> 10:26.232
Okay. So hypothetical 1.

10:28.196 --> 10:31.577
Planning Commissioner A meets with Planning Commissioner B

10:32.077 --> 10:35.909
to discuss a preliminary plat application coming before the Planning Commission on the next agenda.

10:35.990 --> 10:39.454
Commissioner A convinces Commissioner B that the preliminary plat does not meet legal

10:39.954 --> 10:42.887
requirements. They decide to vote against it the next meeting. Is this a violation of

10:43.387 --> 10:44.296
the Open Public Meetings Act?

10:47.046 --> 10:50.409
Yes. Yes. In spirit,

10:50.991 --> 10:54.386
probably. No. No. It is not. It's not 3.

10:54.709 --> 10:58.972
They are not a quorum. So that is the key issue here to highlight.

11:00.279 --> 11:03.733
It is okay, again, Ideally, again, the spirit,

11:03.814 --> 11:06.889
you gotta remember the spirit of the Act is that these things occur in a

11:07.389 --> 11:10.729
public meeting, in the open. However, it is not against

11:10.761 --> 11:14.849
the law for one of you to discuss with another one of you issues

11:14.881 --> 11:17.375
coming on the upcoming agenda, if you want.

11:19.016 --> 11:22.404
Even if you decide, hey, I know this is what I'm gonna do, like,

11:22.727 --> 11:25.857
you should, I think, you know, as legal counsel, I would advise

11:25.873 --> 11:29.158
you to keep an open mind and to see seek

11:29.658 --> 11:32.988
advice and all that and discuss and not make decisions in

11:33.488 --> 11:36.817
advance of the meeting. That being said, I'm here to instruct you on

11:37.317 --> 11:41.160
the law, and what the law says is that if it's

11:41.660 --> 11:45.262
not a quorum, then it's technically lawful, you know, in this situation.

11:45.342 --> 11:47.890
So, okay, hypothetical 2.

11:48.835 --> 11:52.264
Commissioner A speaks to Commissioner B about a rezoning application in front of the Planning

11:52.296 --> 11:56.368
Commission next week. He convinces Commissioner B Excuse

11:56.384 --> 11:59.724
me, I used the male gender in all of these,

12:00.799 --> 12:04.460
so I guess, you know, males tend to be up to no good

12:04.492 --> 12:06.564
more often, so. No,

12:08.025 --> 12:11.397
he convinces Commissioner B to recommend approval. Commissioner B speaks to

12:11.897 --> 12:15.026
Commissioner C to get his support. Commissioner C dislikes the rezone application,

12:15.090 --> 12:18.221
declines to give support. Is this a violation of the Open and Public Meetings Act?

12:21.772 --> 12:25.290
So the trickier this year, so— It involves 3, but it

12:25.790 --> 12:28.836
doesn't involve all 3 at the same time, right?

12:31.019 --> 12:34.043
So technically it's not a violation,

12:34.237 --> 12:37.649
but it feels weird. Would be. I probably wouldn't

12:38.149 --> 12:40.840
do it. Yeah. I would say no, it's not a violation.

12:41.910 --> 12:44.453
Anybody else? Agree, disagree,

12:45.085 --> 12:48.809
don't know? Okay, let's go back

12:49.309 --> 12:52.919
to the— okay. Individuals constituting a quorum of

12:53.419 --> 12:56.307
a public body may not act together outside. So we have 3 individuals here,

12:56.355 --> 12:59.470
so that would be a quorum. May not act together outside a meeting in a

12:59.970 --> 13:03.741
concerted and deliberate way to predetermine an action to be taken by the public body

13:04.241 --> 13:08.125
at a meeting on a relevant matter. So again, I would say that this is

13:08.157 --> 13:11.159
a violation, even though they don't necessarily all come to the same conclusion.

13:11.593 --> 13:14.832
They did meet with the intent to predetermine an action to be taken by the

13:15.332 --> 13:17.476
public body, and they did constitute a quorum.

13:19.105 --> 13:23.253
And this section of code specifically contemplates

13:23.753 --> 13:24.802
not all together at one time.

13:27.136 --> 13:30.030
So it is actually, I think, a violation. It's a little bit more of a

13:30.530 --> 13:34.168
gray area because they don't actually predetermine the action to be taken.

13:34.573 --> 13:38.253
They only attempt to do so. But I do think that that would actually be

13:38.753 --> 13:42.056
a Technical violation, and in addition to a violation of the spirit of the Act.

13:43.432 --> 13:44.904
Okay. Hypothetical 3.

13:47.169 --> 13:50.565
Planning Commissioner A texts Planning Commissioner B about a variance request

13:51.065 --> 13:54.204
on next week's agenda, and they decide over text messaging that they will vote

13:54.704 --> 13:57.968
to deny the request. Planning Commissioner B texts Planning Commissioner C to solicit

13:57.984 --> 14:01.758
his vote. Planning Commissioner C texts back that he also opposes the variances.

14:01.855 --> 14:03.000
Is this a violation?

14:06.323 --> 14:10.268
Basically, yes. Yes, again, the point here is that

14:10.300 --> 14:13.938
text messaging doesn't change anything. It could be over phone calls, it could be

14:14.438 --> 14:17.871
in person, it could be emails. Text messaging just

14:17.919 --> 14:20.425
makes it easier to prove the violation because it's in writing.

14:22.142 --> 14:23.212
So, all right.

14:25.709 --> 14:26.925
Let's talk ethics.

14:27.914 --> 14:31.125
Okay, Municipal Officers and Employees Ethics Act.

14:31.380 --> 14:34.567
Planning commissioners are municipal officers under this act.

14:35.230 --> 14:38.692
Okay, the purpose of this part is— the purposes

14:39.192 --> 14:41.889
of this part are to establish standards of conduct for municipal officers and employees,

14:42.002 --> 14:45.353
and to require these persons to disclose actual or potential

14:45.385 --> 14:48.462
conflicts of interest between their public duties and their personal

14:48.768 --> 14:51.866
interests. Okay.

14:52.934 --> 14:56.574
Definition of appointed officer. So the first question is, are you subject to

14:57.074 --> 15:00.099
the act? And I already told you the answer to this question. Yes, any person

15:00.599 --> 15:04.076
appointed to any statutory officer position

15:04.205 --> 15:07.300
or any other person appointed to any position of employment with the city or with

15:07.316 --> 15:08.968
committee. Da da da da da.

15:10.443 --> 15:14.067
And it says, including appointed officers include but are not limited

15:14.567 --> 15:17.227
to persons serving on special, regular, or full-time committees,

15:17.355 --> 15:21.092
agencies, or boards, whether or not such persons are compensated for their services.

15:21.733 --> 15:24.299
Planning Commission does meet that definition of.

15:24.953 --> 15:26.897
full-time committees, agencies, or boards.

15:27.989 --> 15:31.941
Special, regular, or full-time committees, agencies, or boards. So definitely a regular

15:32.166 --> 15:34.771
committee— it would be a regular committee, agency, or board.

15:35.295 --> 15:35.836
So there you go.

15:39.073 --> 15:42.432
Okay. It is unlawful to disclose or improperly use private, controlled,

15:42.448 --> 15:46.129
or protected information acquired by reason of your position. This is very

15:46.162 --> 15:49.429
unlikely to ever occur, but theoretically possible,

15:49.445 --> 15:54.803
so we're going to talk about it. To further substantially the

15:54.835 --> 15:58.172
officer's personal economic interests, or to secure

15:58.672 --> 16:02.197
special privileges or exemptions for the officer or employee, or for others.

16:04.038 --> 16:07.756
Okay. It is highly unlikely that you will ever come into contact with

16:07.788 --> 16:10.073
private controller protected information under GRAMA.

16:12.944 --> 16:16.784
Theoretically possible, so, But if

16:16.832 --> 16:20.438
you do, obviously you don't use that information to your

16:20.648 --> 16:22.799
personal advantage. That would be unethical.

16:25.430 --> 16:28.681
Okay. These glasses are kind of getting in the way, but it

16:29.181 --> 16:32.985
is unlawful to knowingly receive, accept, take, seek, or solicit directly

16:33.485 --> 16:36.724
or indirectly for the officer or employee or for another

16:37.254 --> 16:41.273
a gift of substantial value, or a substantial

16:41.773 --> 16:45.514
economic benefit tantamount to a gift. That would tend to improperly—

16:47.544 --> 16:51.683
would tend improperly to influence

16:52.183 --> 16:56.160
a reasonable person in the person's position to depart from the faithful and impartial

16:56.193 --> 17:00.010
discharge of the person's duties. The person knows, or that

17:00.075 --> 17:03.610
a reasonable person in that position should know, under the circumstances, primarily for

17:04.110 --> 17:07.624
the purpose of rewarding the person for official action taken. And then

17:08.059 --> 17:11.267
there's the kind of safe harbor Occasional gifts,

17:11.705 --> 17:15.901
$50 and under, are considered presumptively not

17:16.403 --> 17:20.380
in violation. So if—

17:22.292 --> 17:26.630
yeah. So I don't know how often people

17:27.130 --> 17:30.993
are giving gifts to planning commissioners. I suppose at Christmas things happen, like candies and

17:31.493 --> 17:34.661
stuff, cookies. We had the opportunity to go on

17:34.758 --> 17:37.395
a helicopter ride. That could have been considered a gift.

17:37.541 --> 17:40.913
Yeah, I think I am aware of the situation to

17:40.945 --> 17:44.799
which you're referring. Yes, that would be considered

17:45.299 --> 17:49.268
a gift because that person or entity offering the helicopter rides

17:50.185 --> 17:52.379
had business before the Planning Commission and the City Council.

17:53.403 --> 17:57.383
And so it could be considered a reward for services rendered.

17:59.193 --> 18:02.440
And so if it was under $50 worth, value, then you

18:02.940 --> 18:05.740
wouldn't have to report it, and it's presumptively okay. If it's over $50, it doesn't

18:06.240 --> 18:09.510
necessarily mean it's not okay, it's just not presumptively okay.

18:10.580 --> 18:13.720
You get into the gray area. The further from $50 you get,

18:14.220 --> 18:17.619
the less gray it gets. So if somebody offers you something,

18:18.119 --> 18:21.300
you know, $10,000 worth of value, it's pretty clear-cut that that would be a violation.

18:21.460 --> 18:25.220
If somebody offers you $51, probably less clear-cut.

18:25.720 --> 18:29.521
It's probably not a violation. Any more than $50 would be. It's where you

18:30.021 --> 18:33.076
draw the line. So,

18:34.206 --> 18:37.242
you know, there's a, you know, when I was a kid, I hate it now,

18:37.451 --> 18:39.276
but when I was a kid, I was a big fan of Law and Order,

18:39.631 --> 18:42.197
and after I became an attorney, I became less of a fan,

18:43.181 --> 18:46.485
because it just drives you nuts, 'cause you understand the law and why they're

18:46.985 --> 18:51.655
getting it wrong. And, but there was an episode where a

18:51.720 --> 18:55.043
person who was a very wealthy individual, this all takes place

18:55.543 --> 18:59.864
in New York City, uses her influence with the bank, 'cause her business does substantial

18:59.992 --> 19:03.362
banking with this bank, to get

19:03.426 --> 19:06.940
a judge a below-market mortgage interest rate

19:07.598 --> 19:09.716
as a way to bribe him. So I think that would be the def— that

19:10.216 --> 19:13.936
would be like the quintessential substantial economic benefit tantamount to a gift, because she's

19:13.952 --> 19:17.562
not actually directly providing it, she's not actually, they're not, no money

19:18.062 --> 19:21.750
is changing hands, he's just getting a significantly lower interest rate

19:22.250 --> 19:24.189
that's gonna save him, you know, thousands of dollars a month on his mortgage.

19:26.324 --> 19:30.402
So, which is certainly something of value and would be a bribe under any version

19:30.902 --> 19:33.741
of the law. But the issue here is,

19:34.175 --> 19:38.156
would a reasonable person under the circumstances think that the purpose

19:38.252 --> 19:41.912
is for rewarding them of official action taking or trying to influence them?

19:42.282 --> 19:46.118
That's kind of the key language. Rule of thumb, stick to the $50 limit.

19:47.178 --> 19:50.597
You know, use your discretion on what you think occasionally if you

19:51.097 --> 19:54.413
have questions as well. Um, okay, another video. This is

19:54.913 --> 19:58.558
another example of, you know, what not to do. Hey,

19:58.638 --> 20:01.520
Tom, check it out. Norton Construction just sent this over.

20:02.281 --> 20:04.935
Whoa, mama! I love Great Lakes wine.

20:05.146 --> 20:08.300
No, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no,

20:08.317 --> 20:11.404
no. You know as well as I do that we are not allowed to accept

20:11.436 --> 20:16.092
gifts over $25 for corruption reasons. We live in a fishbowl and

20:16.205 --> 20:19.457
the public is always watching. But you cannot enjoy this basket. I'm so

20:19.957 --> 20:21.972
sorry. And so to avoid temptation, I'm going to lock it up.

20:25.750 --> 20:28.502
Okay. So apparently in Pawnee,

20:28.599 --> 20:31.741
Indiana, it's $25 is their limit.

20:32.065 --> 20:35.040
But again, I think, you know, again,

20:35.379 --> 20:39.138
under these circumstances where it is a gift that

20:39.268 --> 20:43.060
is probably slightly over $50, it's probably okay.

20:43.338 --> 20:46.194
especially when it's given to a big group of people and not just one individual.

20:46.548 --> 20:50.469
But that was not the one I was thinking of. That comes

20:50.485 --> 20:54.414
up later. Ooh. But in any

20:54.430 --> 20:57.586
event, yes. If somebody

20:58.086 --> 21:01.154
wants to, again, Christmas holiday gifts kind of thing, that's fine. Just,

21:02.257 --> 21:05.420
you know, make sure that they're not like wining and dining you

21:05.436 --> 21:08.600
every week. I don't think that's remotely likely, but.

21:10.515 --> 21:14.303
It is something to be aware of. So it is a responsibility you have under

21:14.803 --> 21:18.671
the Ethics Act. Okay, it is unlawful to use or attempt to use your

21:18.720 --> 21:22.209
official position to further substantially your economic interest

21:22.451 --> 21:26.025
or secure special privileges. This is another,

21:27.218 --> 21:30.733
I don't anticipate this will be a problem, but it's

21:30.862 --> 21:33.329
obvious, and I think it's common sense, but it's something to be aware of.

21:33.458 --> 21:35.893
Okay, let's, Let's go to the next one.

21:40.077 --> 21:43.004
There's a million ways to exploit your power, but have I ever given in to

21:43.504 --> 21:47.371
that temptation? No, never. I'm not that kind of politician. Mr. Parks and Rec Business,

21:47.533 --> 21:50.342
just need to grab a quick hot dog. Sorry about that,

21:50.423 --> 21:53.402
little girl, you can have the next one. I have a moral code and I

21:53.902 --> 21:57.445
never violate it. People look at me and they say, wow, Tom, you are so

21:57.945 --> 22:02.425
pure. You're like a brown Superman with a beard that just stands for

22:06.388 --> 22:10.288
Okay. Yeah, it's, you know, obviously nobody would be that brazen,

22:10.804 --> 22:12.673
hopefully. But yes,

22:13.866 --> 22:16.913
that's an example of what not to do. Okay.

22:16.993 --> 22:21.415
Violations of the Act can be prosecuted criminally.

22:22.077 --> 22:25.441
So that is one of the potential consequences for violation. Depending on the value of

22:25.941 --> 22:29.119
any benefit received, it can be up to a second-degree felony. felony, anywhere from a

22:29.345 --> 22:33.369
B misdemeanor to a second-degree felony. Again, using the $10,000

22:33.869 --> 22:37.481
example, that would be a second-degree felony. Using the $51 example, that would be a

22:37.981 --> 22:41.742
B misdemeanor, and anywhere in between. So just

22:42.242 --> 22:45.069
don't do it, not a problem. Yes?

22:46.804 --> 22:50.097
It's a total hypothetical, but if

22:50.113 --> 22:53.825
someone were to deliver a gift to your porch, say, like in that

22:53.882 --> 22:56.130
It shows up on their desk, then what?

22:57.094 --> 23:00.337
Okay, so if somebody were to deliver a gift that,

23:00.771 --> 23:03.918
you know, clearly has a value, or, you know, you are aware that it has

23:04.418 --> 23:07.691
a value— And felt uncomfortable receiving because it's— That is above $50.

23:08.109 --> 23:11.673
Right. Yeah, then I would

23:12.412 --> 23:15.639
report it. To you? To— Yeah, I mean,

23:15.703 --> 23:17.438
you can reach out to me, reach out to Brandon.

23:19.330 --> 23:23.420
And, you know, we'll advise you on what to do. I think this is

23:23.920 --> 23:27.280
unlikely to occur. I think it's remote. I don't think anyone even knows I'm

23:27.780 --> 23:31.300
on the panel. But if somebody says, you know, here's, you know,

23:31.800 --> 23:35.000
they give you like 2 tickets to the Jazz game and say, all right,

23:35.500 --> 23:38.940
you know, looking forward to seeing you at Planning Commission next week. You know,

23:39.440 --> 23:42.960
if somebody's that brazen, yeah, I mean, just please let us

23:43.460 --> 23:46.554
know. You know, depending on, Depending on what it is exactly,

23:46.780 --> 23:50.412
we would respond appropriately. And like in that scenario, I'd probably

23:50.912 --> 23:54.730
report it to law enforcement, 'cause that's somebody's attempting to bribe a planning commissioner and

23:55.230 --> 23:58.567
take official action. You know, in another

23:59.067 --> 24:02.403
situation where it's a gift basket like there, but it's a really nice one and

24:02.903 --> 24:06.030
you just don't feel comfortable accepting it, then we would deal with that

24:06.530 --> 24:10.229
probably differently. Okay, just curious and adding some interest to

24:10.729 --> 24:14.346
the conversation. Yeah. Yeah, please. If you have questions, feel free to interrupt.

24:14.718 --> 24:17.142
I mean, as much as I love hearing the sound of my own voice,

24:17.158 --> 24:19.308
I'd much prefer to hear the sound of your voices.

24:22.192 --> 24:23.405
So, okay. So,

24:26.333 --> 24:30.265
yeah, so I mean, under the state law, under the Municipal Employees

24:31.173 --> 24:34.529
Ethics Act, the big

24:34.578 --> 24:38.387
thing that you have to do aside from avoiding accepting gifts of large amounts,

24:39.382 --> 24:43.011
is to report certain things that present

24:43.511 --> 24:46.993
a potential conflict of interest. Okay, so again,

24:48.149 --> 24:52.163
in typical legalese, we start with, it is an offense,

24:52.228 --> 24:55.423
i.e., it is a crime, for a planning commission member to

24:55.455 --> 24:59.148
receive or agree to receive compensation for assisting any person or business

24:59.648 --> 25:02.921
entity in any transaction involving the municipality in which the member is an officer,

25:02.970 --> 25:06.484
unless you first file with

25:06.984 --> 25:10.257
the mayor a sworn statement giving the name and address of

25:10.289 --> 25:13.530
yourself, the name and address of the person

25:14.030 --> 25:16.163
or business entity that you'll be assisting,

25:18.687 --> 25:22.889
and a brief description of the transaction. So, you know,

25:23.050 --> 25:26.850
again, this would,

25:27.382 --> 25:30.774
I think, normally occur during a context where somebody has

25:30.791 --> 25:34.067
hired you in the, you know, again, you're laypeople, you're not like

25:34.164 --> 25:37.376
professional planning commissioners where this is your job. You all have

25:37.876 --> 25:41.632
day jobs. And so this would most

25:42.132 --> 25:45.487
likely occur in a situation where somebody has hired you in the course of

25:45.987 --> 25:49.085
your professional duties unrelated to being a planning commissioner, and they want,

25:49.680 --> 25:53.567
in which case you just have to disclose it and then recuse yourself

25:53.615 --> 25:57.628
from any involvement in that action. So that's pretty straightforward.

25:58.590 --> 26:01.687
Under state law, actually, you're only required to disclose it.

26:02.666 --> 26:05.778
Under city law, you're also required to recuse from being

26:05.810 --> 26:09.163
involved in the action. So yeah,

26:09.179 --> 26:12.725
so there's a disclosure form that you can get from the city recorder

26:13.415 --> 26:16.720
if you have that type of, any type of conflicts or any

26:17.220 --> 26:19.335
required disclosures. You should be filling those out, I think, annually.

26:20.346 --> 26:24.513
So. Okay, planning commissioner

26:24.641 --> 26:27.782
who is an officer, director, agent, or employee, or the owner of

26:28.282 --> 26:31.916
a substantial interest in any business entity which is subject to regulation of the municipality,

26:32.605 --> 26:36.035
so in which you are a planning commission member,

26:36.419 --> 26:39.720
you must disclose the position held and the nature and value

26:40.220 --> 26:43.486
of your interest upon first becoming appointed. Again, there's a disclosure form.

26:44.143 --> 26:47.477
So if, for example, you are a business owner and you have a

26:47.977 --> 26:51.493
business, and that business is based in Eagle Mountain, and thus you

26:51.993 --> 26:55.878
have a business license with Eagle Mountain, for example, maybe you own

26:56.890 --> 26:59.990
Crumbl Cookies or something, you own a franchise

27:00.086 --> 27:03.523
there, and you get a business license, and you're subject to regulation by the city

27:03.636 --> 27:07.154
and all that zoning and all that good stuff, then you would just

27:07.654 --> 27:11.555
simply disclose that interest. And if by chance that Crumbl Cookie

27:11.587 --> 27:15.167
franchise had business before the Planning Commission, you would then recuse from any

27:15.199 --> 27:18.842
action related to that. So that's pretty— anytime,

27:18.906 --> 27:21.827
say you're a 50% owner and then you buy out your partner and you become

27:22.327 --> 27:25.840
100% owner, you would disclose that change as well.

27:27.075 --> 27:30.398
Again, we have a form. It says an interest of $2,000 or less need not

27:30.430 --> 27:33.784
be disclosed. So pretty straightforward,

27:34.057 --> 27:38.069
and when in doubt, disclose is always my advice. I get this

27:38.569 --> 27:42.557
question much more often from city council members. But again, my advice

27:42.671 --> 27:45.911
is pretty consistent that, you know, I always advise

27:45.928 --> 27:49.562
to err on the side of disclosure. Like, it may be

27:50.062 --> 27:53.540
something that you don't technically legally have to disclose, but then

27:54.040 --> 27:57.825
it's just like, oh look, if they're disclosing that, then they should, you know,

27:58.020 --> 27:59.649
they'll disclose something that's worse than that.

28:02.854 --> 28:06.683
Planning Commission member who is an officer, director, agent, employee, or owner of substantial interest

28:07.183 --> 28:10.473
in any business which does or anticipates doing business with the municipality in which he's

28:10.973 --> 28:14.738
a Planning Commission member shall publicly disclose to the members of the Planning Commission immediately

28:15.238 --> 28:17.577
prior to any discussion by such body. So we do this every meeting.

28:18.346 --> 28:21.393
We have conflict of interest disclosures. It's right at the

28:21.893 --> 28:25.242
beginning where you would take the opportunity to say,

28:25.723 --> 28:29.379
yes, I own the Crumbl Cookies. That's agenda

28:29.395 --> 28:32.779
item 7B on tonight's agenda, and so I will

28:33.279 --> 28:35.912
be recusing from that. That would be an example.

28:37.110 --> 28:38.961
So that's pretty straightforward. Any questions?

28:40.362 --> 28:44.290
Yes. Mike, if you're

28:44.790 --> 28:48.224
not financially involved with a business, but you're

28:48.322 --> 28:51.797
heavily emotionally involved with the business, would you have to

28:52.056 --> 28:55.855
recuse yourself if it came before the Planning Commission? I guess I

28:56.355 --> 28:59.444
want to understand what you're saying a little more. Like, you really like Crumbl cookies,

28:59.543 --> 29:03.080
and And so— Right, like if it's something that people want to bring to

29:03.580 --> 29:06.617
the city and you really want to support it and promote it,

29:08.172 --> 29:12.698
can you— So again, it requires some sort of either employer-employee

29:13.580 --> 29:16.823
relationship or agency relationship,

29:16.840 --> 29:20.632
like you've contracted with them or you own it in some

29:21.132 --> 29:24.449
fashion, or that would be the type of relationship we're talking about.

29:24.813 --> 29:28.262
So you're saying, man, I really, want Chick-fil-A to come to Eagle

29:28.762 --> 29:32.710
Mountain City, and this business, this Chick-fil-A owner,

29:33.096 --> 29:35.730
is wanting a business license, or whatever. I'm trying to think of something that would

29:36.230 --> 29:38.123
come before the Planning Commission. They're asking for a rezone,

29:39.504 --> 29:42.539
so that they can open up a Chick-fil-A. And you say, I love Chick-fil-A,

29:42.555 --> 29:45.254
and I think every city should have as many Chick-fil-A's as possible,

29:45.767 --> 29:49.333
and I, you know, like, 'cause it's just the best. Their chicken is awesome.

29:50.666 --> 29:54.455
Would that require disclosure? No. Or recusal,

29:54.471 --> 29:57.553
no. It would have to be some sort of, again,

29:58.612 --> 30:01.934
officer, director, agent, so employee,

30:02.336 --> 30:05.626
or owner. Something of that nature where you

30:05.674 --> 30:09.028
have some sort of financial interest. So assisting

30:09.125 --> 30:12.527
them in the proper pathways, and this is who you

30:13.027 --> 30:16.588
need to talk to, this is what you need to do. Again— Involvement, but not

30:17.088 --> 30:20.149
financial. Yeah, I mean, it depends on the nature of that assistance. Are they paying

30:20.247 --> 30:24.261
you for that assistance? Again, because we just covered that a couple slides

30:24.761 --> 30:28.073
ago. If they're not and they're

30:28.089 --> 30:31.520
just saying, hey, I know you're on the Planning Commission, I have some questions about,

30:31.697 --> 30:34.467
you know, the process, you know, I want to locate here,

30:35.798 --> 30:38.075
you know, and you say, oh yeah, you can look at the zoning map,

30:38.092 --> 30:41.418
see what it's zoned for on the city's website, here's how you navigate to there,

30:42.032 --> 30:45.333
and then if you need a rezone, you know, you file an application, you can

30:45.833 --> 30:49.216
get a You can contact Community Development and they'll assist

30:49.716 --> 30:53.303
you with the application process for rezone so that you can get the correct zoning

30:53.335 --> 30:57.464
to open up that Chick-fil-A. And that

30:57.964 --> 31:01.772
type of situation, that's fine. People ask me

31:01.916 --> 31:05.624
legal questions all the time. I always say, I'm not your lawyer, but here's

31:05.656 --> 31:09.260
some friendly advice. Like, this is kind of— this is what you want

31:09.760 --> 31:11.771
to— kinds of things that you would talk to your lawyer about.

31:13.989 --> 31:15.591
So, who is not me.

31:20.208 --> 31:23.561
Drew Corsey, you had a question? Yeah, you may be getting to this,

31:23.740 --> 31:26.576
but familial, like say it was a, I don't know,

31:26.721 --> 31:29.485
brother-in-law that wants to open up the Chick-fil-A.

31:30.115 --> 31:33.445
Yeah, so that

31:33.945 --> 31:36.679
would be, I think, affected under, let's go back to,

31:38.394 --> 31:41.493
Okay. Special privileges.

31:41.990 --> 31:45.378
Yeah, this, where you're talking about—

31:45.878 --> 31:48.798
go back one more slide. There we go.

31:49.858 --> 31:53.728
Getting substantial value, you know, it says again, for the

31:54.228 --> 31:58.593
employee or for another, right, is what it's talking about. So again,

31:58.769 --> 32:01.997
I would disclose that, because it could be

32:02.061 --> 32:05.303
perceived that is a benefit to a family member,

32:05.752 --> 32:09.186
which meets the definition of a benefit for you or another.

32:09.924 --> 32:13.261
Again, somebody related closely to you in this case.

32:14.175 --> 32:17.849
And say, I would— so what I would do is I would not— I would

32:18.026 --> 32:21.636
recuse from that and disclose it. Say, hey, look, this person is my brother or

32:22.136 --> 32:25.711
whatever. You know, I haven't talked to him at all about this,

32:25.887 --> 32:29.511
but I'm going to disclose that, and I'm going to recuse from any consideration.

32:30.028 --> 32:33.577
That's how I would handle that situation specifically,

32:34.222 --> 32:37.729
because I do think that that is potentially problematic. Yeah,

32:37.793 --> 32:42.100
just for the record, I don't have any family in Eagle Mountain, but just asking.

32:42.293 --> 32:45.636
In that case, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah. Somebody moves

32:45.668 --> 32:48.767
here, you never know. Yeah, I was saying, at some point we've all fallen for

32:49.267 --> 32:52.783
the trick. Question on, in that

32:53.283 --> 32:57.848
context, like, so there's a formal disclosure form

32:57.993 --> 33:01.422
that you would submit ahead of time, but like if it's just—

33:01.922 --> 33:05.284
In theory, there should be an annual disclosure that you're filing. Oh,

33:05.588 --> 33:09.178
okay. So if that is not occurring, we can make sure that that does

33:09.210 --> 33:12.703
occur going forward. Okay. I have— I don't—

33:12.815 --> 33:16.341
I haven't seen the annual one. I don't know. Did you file a disclosure form

33:16.841 --> 33:20.267
when you first were appointed? I have to— I'm pretty sure I did. Yeah,

33:20.347 --> 33:23.424
okay. So yeah, I'll have to look at it whether or not it's just the

33:23.924 --> 33:26.900
City Council has to update it annually if possible. Okay. I know, I mean,

33:26.997 --> 33:30.312
it's clear that you have to update it if things change. Yeah. But it may

33:30.456 --> 33:33.683
just be that you do it when you're first appointed. Okay. I know the City

33:34.183 --> 33:36.668
Council has to do it every year, but that may not actually apply to you.

33:37.863 --> 33:39.735
So, okay.

33:42.145 --> 33:46.658
Okay, again, any personal interest or investment which

33:46.690 --> 33:50.470
creates conflict between you and your public duties, is disclosed in

33:50.502 --> 33:54.133
this meeting, or, you know, in the next meeting, and you

33:54.197 --> 33:56.463
also disclose it in a sworn statement to the mayor.

33:57.427 --> 34:00.479
So again, we have that at the beginning of every meeting, any conflict of

34:00.979 --> 34:04.255
interest, and routinely people say no, 'cause that's usually the case.

34:04.962 --> 34:08.609
And yes, but in the event that somebody says yes,

34:08.995 --> 34:12.899
you would describe the conflict, you'd fill out the form, submit it, okay, and give

34:12.915 --> 34:15.325
it to Brandon. He'd make sure the mayor gets it. Thank you.

34:18.929 --> 34:23.017
Okay. In addition to any criminal penalties that we've previously discussed,

34:23.828 --> 34:27.235
violation of disclosure requirements mandates

34:27.735 --> 34:29.695
expulsion from the Planning Commission. I mean, it seems pretty self-explanatory.

34:31.737 --> 34:32.751
Don't break the ethical rules.

34:35.989 --> 34:39.890
It also— if any action taken in violation of disclosure requirements

34:40.390 --> 34:42.139
could potentially— the action could potentially voided.

34:44.581 --> 34:47.912
So we just want to steer clear of

34:48.412 --> 34:51.844
any of that. So my advice is, when in doubt, disclose,

34:52.509 --> 34:55.718
disclose, disclose. I think that's the safest course.

34:56.982 --> 34:59.267
If you say, all right, you know, we have a rezone application,

35:01.176 --> 35:05.152
and, you know, my family

35:05.201 --> 35:08.684
member owns property in that area

35:09.184 --> 35:13.543
that's being rezoned. They don't really have any plans to do anything differently,

35:13.831 --> 35:16.301
but, you know, I mean, like,

35:17.247 --> 35:20.069
anything as innocuous as that, I would say that's probably not legally required to be

35:20.569 --> 35:23.773
disclosed, but, you know, so if you have any questions,

35:23.821 --> 35:27.253
should I disclose it or not, my advice is always just disclose

35:27.301 --> 35:30.492
it. I mean, anybody's gonna think, oh yeah, you know.

35:31.198 --> 35:34.613
If you disclose and you don't, strictly speaking, have to disclose, then you can

35:35.113 --> 35:38.897
still still vote if you want to, and we can have that discussion about

35:38.946 --> 35:42.067
whether or not disclosure is strictly speaking required,

35:43.247 --> 35:46.352
but you still wanna vote and participate 'cause that's your duty as a member of

35:46.852 --> 35:49.444
the Planning Commission. And that's fine,

35:50.188 --> 35:53.615
that does happen. But again,

35:53.647 --> 35:57.042
in that situation, I wouldn't think you would be required to disclose, in my opinion.

35:57.171 --> 36:00.020
So I would say you could still vote even if you disclosed it. Yeah,

36:00.036 --> 36:02.432
I just had a follow-up question on that. So if you had, like, in an

36:02.480 --> 36:06.429
agenda, Somebody, as we're doing your conflict

36:06.929 --> 36:10.216
of interest, if somebody notes that they have a conflict for a particular item,

36:10.826 --> 36:14.098
what's the proceed— can they be part of the discussion,

36:14.371 --> 36:17.837
or— So my preference is that if you

36:18.337 --> 36:22.152
think your answer to that question might be yes, is to contact me in advance

36:22.652 --> 36:25.570
of the meeting, and we can have a discussion not in a public setting.

36:25.827 --> 36:29.212
Okay, the nature of— Yeah, so we can have an attorney-client

36:30.150 --> 36:32.905
Okay. And about the issue.

36:33.163 --> 36:36.499
Okay. Is that— I don't know if anybody's had a situation like that

36:36.692 --> 36:40.336
come up on— So— I've never had that situation come up on a meeting that

36:40.836 --> 36:44.776
I've attended, but it's good to know individually. Yeah, I mean, if, for example,

36:44.857 --> 36:49.374
like something like UDOT comes in and they are doing something and

36:49.633 --> 36:52.788
it actually relates to your responsibilities with UDOT, then yeah.

36:53.111 --> 36:56.299
Okay. Sounds good. We'll have that discussion.

37:01.152 --> 37:03.588
Okay. Okay, so this is— now we're getting into city code.

37:05.490 --> 37:09.147
So again, here, this again, this is going to your question a little bit

37:09.647 --> 37:13.827
about siblings. But basically it says, for purposes of this chapter, an official

37:14.327 --> 37:17.029
has a conflict of interest if the official knows or has reason to know that

37:17.529 --> 37:21.139
the official or a parent of the official or the official's spouse, sibling, or other

37:21.639 --> 37:25.089
member of the same household, agent, principal, client, employer, or employee,

37:25.655 --> 37:29.104
is required to disclose a conflict of interest by the requirements of the

37:29.604 --> 37:33.716
Municipal Officers and Employees Ethics Act. And then adds additional requirements.

37:33.765 --> 37:37.806
So again, so getting back to the sibling question, under city code

37:38.306 --> 37:40.354
you would have to disclose. I think under state law you would have to as

37:40.549 --> 37:44.100
well. But there's a— it's,

37:44.262 --> 37:48.143
I think, crystal clear under city code. Code has an ownership

37:48.643 --> 37:50.776
interest in a business having matters under proper consideration.

37:51.917 --> 37:55.304
Has an ownership interest in property within Eagle Mountain

37:55.804 --> 37:58.564
City for which zoning, conditional use, or development approvals are under consideration.

37:59.897 --> 38:03.493
Represents as agent, professional services contractor, other advisor,

38:03.814 --> 38:07.202
individual, or organization under consideration before

38:07.702 --> 38:11.072
the Planning Commission. I'm seeing that I copied and pasted this from

38:11.104 --> 38:13.602
the city code. and it's driving me nuts because there's no Oxford comma.

38:14.950 --> 38:18.798
But yes.

38:19.475 --> 38:23.179
Has an ownership interest in a business or property that has been issued

38:23.679 --> 38:26.243
a business license, is a place of business, or otherwise operates a business offering or

38:26.743 --> 38:30.908
providing goods, products, or services in Eagle Mountain, or uses or develops property.

38:31.550 --> 38:34.986
So again, this is all kind of, I think, common sense. If you

38:35.486 --> 38:39.035
have a financial interest in something that has Or somebody that has

38:39.628 --> 38:43.336
business before the Planning Commission, then you should

38:43.836 --> 38:47.172
disclose and recuse. Again, if it's

38:47.672 --> 38:51.794
your primary residence and it's rezoned, that's an exception. But otherwise,

38:53.094 --> 38:56.624
if it's a rental property that's getting rezoned, then yes,

38:56.640 --> 38:59.786
you have to disclose that. It's a business that

38:59.931 --> 39:03.429
has whatever business before the Planning Commission.

39:03.880 --> 39:07.298
You have to disclose, and under our code, if you have to disclose, again,

39:07.314 --> 39:10.392
if you're required to disclose, then you have to recuse as well.

39:14.728 --> 39:18.146
So again, has an ownership interest in property other than that of a primary residence

39:18.646 --> 39:21.774
that might enjoy direct and immediate financial benefit or suffer direct economic harm

39:21.790 --> 39:23.459
by zoning, conditional use, or development approvals.

39:25.110 --> 39:29.309
So by even if the land is not actually owned by you. So for example.

39:29.794 --> 39:33.684
So if somebody wants to open up a gravel pit close to your residence,

39:33.732 --> 39:37.054
you know, 1,000 feet away, like the bare minimum away

39:37.298 --> 39:40.659
that it has to be, or somebody wants to open some

39:41.159 --> 39:45.159
sort of commercial business that might be considered,

39:45.596 --> 39:49.233
you know, something that would devalue your property, sexually oriented business,

39:49.249 --> 39:51.640
for example. Then,

39:55.517 --> 39:59.465
and it's not your primary residence, right, then that would— then you'd

39:59.965 --> 40:03.075
have to disclose and recuse. So you have a business next to this,

40:05.140 --> 40:08.375
you own a property that you, you know, whatever, in a business

40:08.875 --> 40:12.114
that's next to this sexually oriented business that wants approval,

40:13.520 --> 40:17.159
then your business is probably going to go down in value if that gets

40:17.659 --> 40:20.847
approved. So that would be an example, you know,

40:20.943 --> 40:23.639
but the reverse is also true. You know,

40:24.280 --> 40:28.307
you own a car repair business, and somebody

40:28.807 --> 40:32.014
wants approval of a car lot right next to it or something. Theoretically, you know,

40:32.078 --> 40:34.853
something that where there's some sort of synergy there. I don't know, that was just

40:35.206 --> 40:38.078
an example I came up with off the top of my head. But anywhere,

40:38.158 --> 40:41.736
you know, something where you're gonna get some sort of synergy from, for your business,

40:42.891 --> 40:47.704
where this other business, you know, that would be an example

40:48.204 --> 40:51.317
of where you would disclose and recuse. Yeah, so again, I mean,

40:51.333 --> 40:54.240
this next one is common sense, I hope.

40:55.091 --> 40:58.254
Has an agreement with the party seeking approval that would financially benefit the public official

40:58.754 --> 41:05.320
if such approval were granted. Okay, yeah, so that's

41:05.820 --> 41:06.797
a bribe, so don't do that.

41:08.002 --> 41:11.904
Hypothetical 1. Planning Commissioner A goes

41:12.404 --> 41:15.597
to a planning conference to receive training on his duties. Company 1 invites Planning Commissioner

41:16.097 --> 41:19.311
A A to dinner and pays. Company 1 has business before

41:19.811 --> 41:22.944
the Planning Commission. The cost of the Planning Commissioner's dinner is $40. Is this a

41:22.992 --> 41:24.203
violation of the Ethics Act?

41:26.507 --> 41:29.609
No. No. That one's pretty easy. Again, without more,

41:31.742 --> 41:35.118
you know, if they're taking you to dinner every night for like a month,

41:35.538 --> 41:37.654
that's different than what we're talking about here.

41:40.628 --> 41:44.642
Again, hypothetical 2, it's the same facts except the dinner costs $75.

41:44.691 --> 41:48.680
Is this a violation of the Ethics Yes, maybe. I like your answer.

41:49.977 --> 41:52.220
As a lawyer, it depends is like my favorite thing to say.

41:53.566 --> 41:57.380
I would say it's probably not a violation, but you're outside the safe harbor.

41:58.229 --> 42:01.962
So I would recommend against doing that, because,

42:02.539 --> 42:06.400
you know, again, where do you draw the line? Is a $75 dinner

42:06.881 --> 42:10.854
a violation? Is that the type of thing that a reasonable person would not faithfully,

42:11.207 --> 42:14.491
you know, impartially discharge their duties as a planning commissioner?

42:15.293 --> 42:18.691
Probably not. Is $100 that number?

42:19.364 --> 42:22.955
$150? $200? Where's the number? I don't know. All I know is

42:22.987 --> 42:25.360
that the further we drift from $50, the more in trouble we're getting.

42:26.033 --> 42:29.367
So my advice is don't do it. Order something

42:29.383 --> 42:33.038
that costs less than $50 so that Brian will be correct.

42:33.086 --> 42:36.341
You're not violating— you know, that would be a violation for going over $75.

42:37.334 --> 42:39.547
You know, we don't want to even get— we don't want to get near that.

42:40.140 --> 42:43.775
So that's my advice. Thank you. Don't do it. Okay.

42:43.839 --> 42:47.381
Planning Commissioner B owns 25% of Company 2.

42:47.995 --> 42:51.973
Company B has business before the Planning Commission. Does Planning Commissioner B have any obligations

42:52.473 --> 42:55.317
under state or city ethics laws? If so, what are those obligations?

43:03.065 --> 43:06.170
They have a part, they have an ownership in the business, so, but I can't

43:06.218 --> 43:10.951
remember I don't know if there was a 25% or 50%, but I mean,

43:11.565 --> 43:14.719
yeah, I would disclose it either way. The language is substantial interest.

43:15.412 --> 43:18.577
Okay. So I would say 25% is substantial.

43:18.900 --> 43:23.501
Okay. So I

43:24.001 --> 43:25.899
would disclose it. Okay?

43:28.374 --> 43:31.122
And you should be disclosing that when you're appointed,

43:31.748 --> 43:33.339
before there's any business before the Planning Commission.

43:35.049 --> 43:38.235
On the night of the Planning Commission, you would then disclose

43:38.267 --> 43:40.607
it again when we say, do you have any conflict of interest? And you say,

43:40.623 --> 43:42.705
yes, I have conflict of interest on item 7A.

43:45.529 --> 43:48.095
And then you would recuse from that item. Thank you.

43:49.015 --> 43:52.290
Okay. All right. So now this is

43:52.790 --> 43:56.500
a new addition to our training, standards for civil discourse. We have not observed

43:57.000 --> 44:00.630
any issues here, thank goodness, but it is something to be trained on and is

44:00.660 --> 44:04.069
related to ethics. Okay, so this

44:04.182 --> 44:07.992
is from, direct from our city code. It is in the chapter on

44:08.008 --> 44:10.958
city council, but is applicable to, by its terms,

44:11.860 --> 44:15.558
elected officials, members of city boards and commissions, and employees in public, in any

44:15.928 --> 44:19.450
city public meetings. So that would include planning commissioners in a planning commission

44:19.950 --> 44:23.475
meeting. Okay, the following standards are intended

44:23.975 --> 44:27.300
to encourage and require Eagle Mountain City Members of

44:27.332 --> 44:30.744
city boards and commissions to meet obligations of civil

44:31.244 --> 44:34.738
discourse to each other and to the public and comply with the requirement

44:35.077 --> 44:38.649
of a statute that just says you

44:39.149 --> 44:42.699
need to create rules for standards of civil discourse. Elected officials,

44:42.715 --> 44:46.335
members of city boards and commissions, and employees are expected to make mutual and

44:46.835 --> 44:49.097
firm commitments to adhere to these standards. Okay.

44:50.889 --> 44:54.214
So what are they? Conduct and language that

44:54.714 --> 44:58.915
is condescending, uncivil, abrasive, abusive, hostile, or obstructive

44:58.931 --> 45:02.926
impedes the fundamental goal of participating in civil discourse and public processes and

45:02.942 --> 45:06.696
the rational, peaceful, and efficient resolution of decisions and

45:06.728 --> 45:10.129
is a violation of this standard. Such conduct tends

45:10.177 --> 45:13.306
to delay and often to deny resolution of issues in the public interest.

45:13.771 --> 45:17.092
So again, I have not observed an issue in the Planning Commission in

45:17.204 --> 45:20.551
relation to this. So, but it is

45:21.051 --> 45:24.124
something to be aware of that we should— again, I think this is common sense,

45:24.236 --> 45:26.232
but we should be civil in the way that we deal with each other,

45:26.312 --> 45:29.130
we deal with the public, we deal with staff, et cetera.

45:31.241 --> 45:34.624
Elected and appointed officials, and again, planning commissioners are

45:35.124 --> 45:38.841
appointed officials, and city employees should exhibit courtesy,

45:39.068 --> 45:42.453
candor, and cooperation in dealing with each other and the public and in

45:42.485 --> 45:46.478
the administration of the city. Yeah, I think that's pretty straightforward.

45:46.944 --> 45:51.215
So don't try to lie or deceive people. You know,

45:51.375 --> 45:54.586
so, and treat people with courtesy

45:55.086 --> 45:58.713
and respect. So elected officials,

45:58.729 --> 46:02.470
members of city boards and commissions, and employees shall not, without an adequate factual

46:02.518 --> 46:05.793
basis, attribute to other persons improper motives, purpose,

46:05.825 --> 46:09.181
or conduct. Each person affiliated— So again, that's,

46:09.600 --> 46:13.413
Again, don't call someone a liar unless you have the goods

46:13.461 --> 46:16.825
to back it up. So if you're gonna,

46:17.211 --> 46:21.664
you know, you can always assume that they just misunderstand

46:22.164 --> 46:24.993
or that they're wrong, not that they're intentionally fabricating things,

46:25.593 --> 46:28.993
unless you know that they are or have at least good evidence to suggest that

46:29.493 --> 46:32.721
they are. Each person affiliated with

46:33.221 --> 46:36.848
the city should avoid hostile, demeaning, Or humiliating conduct in words

46:37.348 --> 46:41.167
and written and oral communications. Neither written submissions nor oral statements or conduct should disparage

46:41.604 --> 46:44.321
the integrity, intelligence, morals, ethics, or personal behavior of others.

46:45.063 --> 46:49.162
Again, you know I think this is common

46:49.211 --> 46:52.729
sense kind of stuff. But again, I have not observed any issues in

46:53.229 --> 46:57.121
relation to this in the planning commission, so I wouldn't be you

46:57.621 --> 46:59.930
know too concerned. Just keep it in mind. You know again, we're going to.

47:00.968 --> 47:03.447
Treat people with respect, you know, courtesy,

47:03.949 --> 47:06.719
and I think everything else takes care of itself.

