01:17:37.066 --> 01:17:40.837
looking for property for my family, specifically for my

01:17:40.853 --> 01:17:44.593
wife who wanted horse property. And so my

01:17:44.625 --> 01:17:48.252
wife, Danae, had the vision that this would be a place that

01:17:48.752 --> 01:17:51.895
we would want to raise our family. And so Cedar Pass Ranch

01:17:52.729 --> 01:17:56.276
was the development before there was a city. And so

01:17:56.822 --> 01:17:59.919
fortunately we got in early and we were able to build our home in '99.

01:18:01.282 --> 01:18:04.615
So I've seen a lot of the growth. I've seen all

01:18:04.745 --> 01:18:08.030
the growth and the pains that we've gone through as a city.

01:18:09.098 --> 01:18:12.888
But we love it here and

01:18:13.406 --> 01:18:17.294
wouldn't change being here. When I

01:18:17.794 --> 01:18:23.077
am the HOA president and when

01:18:23.674 --> 01:18:26.897
we found out the situation with the zoning, And in

01:18:27.397 --> 01:18:30.622
conversation with Robert Hobbs, we felt like, how can we help

01:18:31.122 --> 01:18:34.569
in this process? And excited

01:18:35.069 --> 01:18:38.521
to learn, not knowing the challenge, but excited to learn that

01:18:39.021 --> 01:18:42.149
we could be a part of it. Then we discovered, wow, what a challenge.

01:18:43.332 --> 01:18:46.727
And so in order to work through this, we decided to

01:18:46.760 --> 01:18:50.826
pull together some really smart

01:18:51.326 --> 01:18:54.323
people within our subdivision who could help and know the history.

01:18:55.396 --> 01:18:58.681
And we put together a committee to help to do this. And the

01:18:59.181 --> 01:19:03.104
committee was John and Susan Morandi with Mayor Burningham,

01:19:03.328 --> 01:19:08.424
Jeff Myers, and myself, working sometimes

01:19:08.680 --> 01:19:11.981
weekly but monthly with Robert over

01:19:12.481 --> 01:19:15.539
a process of hardcore 10 months.

01:19:16.308 --> 01:19:18.519
But this has been almost 2 and a half years in the works.

01:19:20.090 --> 01:19:23.888
So this is hopefully finding a finish line

01:19:24.641 --> 01:19:28.246
in the process. Why don't you add a few things if you like, Susan?

01:19:29.272 --> 01:19:32.301
Yeah, I just want to add that I think the intention of— You can,

01:19:32.637 --> 01:19:35.842
yeah, if you'll step up, yeah. Hi. Tell us who

01:19:36.342 --> 01:19:39.992
you are. Susan Morandi. Okay, thanks. And how long have you been here? How long

01:19:40.008 --> 01:19:43.277
have I been here? Yeah. 9 years.

01:19:43.470 --> 01:19:46.665
I've been in Eagle Mountain 9 years. now. So, um,

01:19:47.939 --> 01:19:54.115
it was— it started as part of the HOA process to— when

01:19:54.147 --> 01:19:58.084
we were years ago looking to rewrite— is when rewrite

01:19:58.584 --> 01:20:01.714
our CC&amp;Rs is when we found out we weren't zoned. I did the research.

01:20:01.956 --> 01:20:04.272
I know we weren't zoned. So,

01:20:05.695 --> 01:20:08.541
um, and the city is kind of— we kind of worked with them trying to

01:20:09.041 --> 01:20:12.768
figure it out and A few years ago, we were approached

01:20:13.268 --> 01:20:17.020
and asked to help create zones, and it was— we were

01:20:17.520 --> 01:20:20.565
tasked with that, and we set up a committee to do it. And it was

01:20:21.065 --> 01:20:24.640
important for everybody to keep a zone with what the residents are

01:20:25.140 --> 01:20:29.100
currently doing now in Cedar Pass Ranch. So we were working along

01:20:29.132 --> 01:20:33.047
that process, and as we were nearing

01:20:33.547 --> 01:20:37.541
the end, then we were asked if we wouldn't mind sharing our zone language

01:20:37.557 --> 01:20:40.946
that we created to use in other parts of the city. And we're like,

01:20:41.717 --> 01:20:45.170
well, we're unique. We don't have the same

01:20:45.267 --> 01:20:48.961
things in other parts, you know, in our development that are

01:20:49.461 --> 01:20:53.668
going to be in other parts of the city, especially the equestrian component

01:20:53.700 --> 01:20:56.976
that— and the trails. That's pretty unique to Cedar Pass Ranch,

01:20:56.993 --> 01:21:00.398
and that's why people are there. So, so that's just a little

01:21:00.446 --> 01:21:04.279
bit of background that we We separated it out into

01:21:05.118 --> 01:21:08.553
the ARLL zone, and then we wanted to kind of keep ours

01:21:08.682 --> 01:21:11.730
more of a historical zone. So, and that's where we're at today.

01:21:12.960 --> 01:21:16.418
Yeah. So as we were reviewing this, I tell you,

01:21:16.889 --> 01:21:20.072
we sure learned a lot. Robert taught us a lot.

01:21:21.968 --> 01:21:24.347
City Attorney helped us work through a lot of the language.

01:21:25.593 --> 01:21:28.336
We actually looked through a lot of the city zones. that are our neighbors.

01:21:31.780 --> 01:21:35.403
The historical part we started to realize

01:21:35.452 --> 01:21:39.188
a few months ago was if you drive—

01:21:39.623 --> 01:21:42.804
we're a very young city. Where would our

01:21:42.853 --> 01:21:46.411
history be with our city? Well, it's in where

01:21:46.911 --> 01:21:50.167
our roots are. And our roots were Cedar Pass Ranch

01:21:50.216 --> 01:21:53.292
and the ranches and the master development plan. Which when

01:21:53.358 --> 01:21:55.664
I saw that, I was like, man, this is a grand plan.

01:21:57.206 --> 01:22:00.830
And wow, how many times did that change? I saw every change.

01:22:01.995 --> 01:22:06.403
And I have been here so many times to witness it.

01:22:07.402 --> 01:22:10.802
It was a great idea, but it had to be adapted over time.

01:22:12.765 --> 01:22:15.553
And when I think about, well, if you drive to Park City, there are some

01:22:15.748 --> 01:22:19.217
really strict zoning in Park City. You can't

01:22:19.249 --> 01:22:23.197
touch downtown Park City. You can't touch downtown Provo.

01:22:23.343 --> 01:22:26.879
You can't change a lot of the history of the older towns.

01:22:27.740 --> 01:22:31.264
And so here, I would hope that the city would be thinking, what are

01:22:31.296 --> 01:22:38.350
our roots? And I remember how

01:22:38.543 --> 01:22:41.766
Eagle Mountain City was advertised, and it

01:22:42.266 --> 01:22:45.337
was footage of Cedar Pass Ranch and the ranches.

01:22:46.144 --> 01:22:49.616
Please come where there's open space, where you

01:22:50.116 --> 01:22:53.669
can grow and you can have animals. And that

01:22:53.814 --> 01:22:57.442
was for years. It was pictures of all of our properties,

01:22:58.529 --> 01:22:59.665
which was great.

01:23:01.944 --> 01:23:05.033
But we feel like that's also the roots of the city. And so we would

01:23:05.098 --> 01:23:08.510
hope that what we work

01:23:09.010 --> 01:23:12.916
towards in recognizing it as a historical zone,

01:23:12.948 --> 01:23:16.078
that the Planning Commission, but also the city council members who

01:23:16.578 --> 01:23:19.536
we've actually spent a lot of time talking with them to get their input so

01:23:20.036 --> 01:23:24.030
that we could do a lot of the

01:23:24.046 --> 01:23:27.144
legwork, a lot of the hard processing.

01:23:28.524 --> 01:23:32.569
And so right towards the tail end, there was some discussion

01:23:33.069 --> 01:23:35.361
about, you know, other parts of the city are going to be developed where we

01:23:35.861 --> 01:23:40.032
would like to do large lots too. Can we take this language and use

01:23:40.532 --> 01:23:43.623
it there? We're like, well, do what you want with it. I think that's great,

01:23:44.059 --> 01:23:47.807
but still there are some significant differences, which is what

01:23:48.307 --> 01:23:51.881
Susan's referred to and which will— Robert's hopefully going to be able

01:23:52.381 --> 01:23:56.151
to go through those key differences so that you see the benefits of both.

01:23:57.850 --> 01:24:01.186
And we're willing to field any questions you have and try to

01:24:01.250 --> 01:24:02.830
help you through this.

01:24:05.390 --> 01:24:09.256
Okay, do you— I guess at this point Just an open discussion

01:24:09.288 --> 01:24:12.752
between commissioners, or do you? Is there more that you want to get

01:24:13.252 --> 01:24:15.760
through to present before we ask questions? Yes.

01:24:16.179 --> 01:24:19.360
Okay. Thank you, Chair, for that question.

01:24:20.280 --> 01:24:23.509
My aspiration is that we would do just as was

01:24:24.009 --> 01:24:27.374
kind of just noted, and be sort of a team approach. And I can present

01:24:27.874 --> 01:24:31.770
a little bit more, and then if there's discussion or questions that either parties,

01:24:31.883 --> 01:24:35.166
self, staff, or the co-applicants,

01:24:35.231 --> 01:24:38.438
if you will, could be involved in that Q&amp;A,

01:24:38.454 --> 01:24:42.321
et cetera. And anecdotally,

01:24:42.370 --> 01:24:46.047
this is— it sounded like from what I've been told in the past when I

01:24:46.112 --> 01:24:50.499
started here that this effort has hit and miss over

01:24:50.709 --> 01:24:52.935
a long span of time, a few times. I don't know how many times.

01:24:53.840 --> 01:24:56.924
To get it to this stage finally is like

01:24:57.424 --> 01:25:01.225
crossing the Olympics, you know, little end ribbon tape in the 100-meter

01:25:01.725 --> 01:25:04.456
dash. So regardless of the outcome,

01:25:05.217 --> 01:25:08.714
our hope is that we'll get a recommendation that council will do

01:25:09.214 --> 01:25:11.466
something with this, that it won't fall by the wayside. That would be tragic.

01:25:14.200 --> 01:25:17.620
So now, in looking at this chart, I think the understanding

01:25:18.120 --> 01:25:21.167
that you heard was that it sounds like the Cedar Pass folks, by and large—

01:25:21.667 --> 01:25:23.827
and this was presented to them at an HOA meeting that they had back in

01:25:24.327 --> 01:25:28.534
May, as I know in Report would probably like to preserve the character

01:25:29.034 --> 01:25:31.614
of what they had run with this most of this time, which is the historic

01:25:32.114 --> 01:25:35.339
zone. But as need be, of course, would be

01:25:35.839 --> 01:25:39.043
open necessarily to what the city might decide if we go with,

01:25:39.059 --> 01:25:41.922
for example, this other ARLL zone. I'll call it for short.

01:25:43.650 --> 01:25:48.089
So starting at the top, these columns

01:25:49.090 --> 01:25:52.206
give you an indication that. There's kind of

01:25:52.706 --> 01:25:56.342
a different purpose statement, obviously, with each. We can kind of skip

